--- body ---
## Context

The ledger's `claim(wid, holder, claimedInput, ttlMs)` acquires a row with a
proper compare-and-swap: `WHERE wid = :wid AND (status = 'pending' OR
(status = 'claimed' AND claim_expires_at <= :now))`. Its two completion
counterparts do not carry the matching half of that contract:

```ts
markDone(wid: number): void {
  this.db.prepare(`UPDATE stage_queue SET status = 'done', ... WHERE wid = ?`).run(wid);
}
markFailed(wid: number, note?: string): void {
  this.db.prepare(`UPDATE stage_queue SET status = 'failed', ... WHERE wid = ?`).run(wid);
}
```

Neither checks who currently holds the row, or even that it is still
`claimed`. So: worker A claims wid, the clock passes the claim TTL,
`releaseExpiredClaims()` (conductor-side sweep, already wired in `poll()`)
releases it, worker B claims the same wid and completes it, and A's own
late completion — genuinely still working, not dead — writes right over
B's, silently. This is exactly the failure ticket 0553's round-2 review
reproduced end-to-end (see its PR #156 comments), diagnosed there against
`ExtractStage`'s own `putExtractState`. The root cause is one level down,
in the shared primitive every future stage (chunk, embed, and 0553 itself
once resumed) will call.

Not yet live: `grep -rn "markDone\|markFailed"` across the fork's
`conductor/` finds only the two definitions, no callers — tranche 3, the
only ticket that would call them, is parked (0553), not merged. This is
prerequisite hardening, not a hotfix for something currently broken in
main.

## Relevant files

- Fork `src/features/search/conductor/ledger.ts` — `claim()` (the CAS
  pattern to mirror), `markDone`/`markFailed` (what needs the same guard),
  `releaseExpiredClaims()` (the sweep that creates the race window).
- Ticket 0553's PR #156 comments (this repo) — the original reproduction,
  against `ExtractStage`/`putExtractState`, for the concrete failure
  sequence and the test shape to reuse (`Ledger`/`ExtractStage` driven by
  `ManualClock`, no real process timing needed).

## Actions

1. Give `markDone`/`markFailed` the same shape `claim()` already has: take
   the `holder` (and ideally `claimed_input`, so a completion can also be
   checked against what was actually claimed, catching a stale worker
   completing against superseded input even within the same holder) and
   guard the UPDATE on `wid = ? AND claimed_by = ?` (`AND claimed_input = ?`
   if that's included). Return whether the write actually applied, the way
   `claim()` returns a boolean today — a caller that gets back "no, someone
   else owns this row now" needs to know, not silently succeed at nothing.
2. Update `ExtractStage` (or wherever ticket 0553 lands its own call sites
   when resumed) to pass its holder id and handle a rejected completion —
   this ticket only needs to exist for 0553 to consume it; wiring the
   caller is 0553's own job, not this ticket's, unless 0553 is resumed
   before this lands, in which case do both together.
3. Purely additive to the ledger's own method signatures where possible;
   if the signature change is breaking, there are no merged callers yet to
   break.

## Test

- Red first: mirror ticket 0553's own reproduction — worker A claims,
  clock advances past TTL, `releaseExpiredClaims()` releases the row,
  worker B claims and completes, then A's own (late) completion call is
  asserted to return false/no-op rather than silently overwriting B's
  result. Use `ManualClock`, no real process timing.
- A second case: A still holds a live (unexpired) claim and completes
  normally — asserts the guard doesn't reject a legitimate completion.

## Verification

- [x] `markDone`/`markFailed` reject a completion from a holder that no
      longer owns the row (claim expired and reclaimed by another holder),
      and reject it when the re-claim carries the same holder and input —
      the case the ticket's own Action 1 would have let through
- [x] A legitimate holder's completion still succeeds, including past an
      unswept TTL
- [x] `claim()`'s existing CAS tests stay green — this ticket touches
      completion, not acquisition. `claim()` keeps its boolean signature
      and delegates to `claimTicket()`, the same statement returning the
      ticket
- [x] Positive control: the guard demonstrably rejects before it is
      trusted — stubbed out, six tests go red and every control arm stays
      green. The sibling branch ran its own, independently: 3 of its 5
      red, its two legitimate-completion arms green.
- [x] The two parallel implementations are reconciled rather than both
      kept — see the log; 0568 is told which side to take

## Invariants

- `claim()` unchanged; only the completion methods gain a check.
- Upstream tests green throughout.

## Exit criteria

- [x] `markDone`/`markFailed` ownership-guarded, on fork branch
      `tranche-3-extract-shim` at `1a38c71`, 1 089 tests green
- [x] Ticket 0553's blocker #1 (claim-race duplicate completion) resolved:
      0553 resumed in the same session and its stage adopted the guard in
      the same commit — see its log
